Q: Convert content:// uri to file:// uri using react native A: <p>You generally can’t “convert” an Android content:// URI into a real filesystem file:// path, because content:// is just a handle served by a ContentProvider. The reliable approach is to copy the content into your app storage and then use that file.</p>
<p>I solved this by creating an Expo Modules native module that:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>accepts a content://... string,</p>
</li>
<li><p>reads it via ContentResolver.openInputStream(),</p>
</li>
<li><p>copies the bytes into context.filesDir/&lt;subdir&gt;/...,</p>
</li>
<li><p>returns the resulting absolute path + name + size.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Link: <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/expo-content-uri" rel="nofollow noreferrer">expo-content-uri </a></p>
<p>Usage (JS/TS):</p>
<pre><code>import { ExpoContentUri } from &quot;expo-content-uri&quot;;
import * as FileSystem from &quot;expo-file-system&quot;;

// contentUri is what DocumentPicker returns on Android (often content://...)
const res = await ExpoContentUri.copyToAppStorage(contentUri, {
  subdir: &quot;imports&quot;,     // optional
  fileName: &quot;my.pdf&quot;,    // optional
});

const filePath = res.filePath; // e.g. /data/user/0/&lt;package&gt;/files/imports/my.pdf

// if a library requires a file:// URI:
const fileUri = &quot;file://&quot; + filePath;

// now use fileUri/filePath with your reader / FileSystem / upload, etc.
</code></pre>
